<SEOArticle
  title="Hip Mobility Routine for Tight Hips"
  description="Try this simple hip mobility routine for tight hips using controlled rotations, lunges, hip flexor work and active range-of-motion exercises."
  canonical="/recovery/hip-mobility-routine-for-tight-hips/"
  eyebrow="Mobility"
  heading="A practical hip mobility routine for tight hips."
  lead="If your hips feel stiff after sitting, lifting or everyday life, a short routine that combines motion and control can be more useful than endlessly stretching the same position."
  {faqs}
  {toc}
  secondaryLabel="10-Minute Full-Body Mobility"
  secondaryHref="/recovery/10-minute-mobility-routine/"
>
  <p><strong>Hip mobility is not about forcing a bigger range.</strong> The goal is to explore comfortable motion, improve control and notice which positions feel easier after a few minutes of deliberate movement.</p>

  <h2 id="routine">10-minute hip mobility routine</h2>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="card"><span class="pill">1 minute</span><h3>90/90 hip switches</h3><p>Move the knees slowly side to side while keeping the feet planted and torso tall enough to stay comfortable.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="pill coral">2 minutes</span><h3>Half-kneeling hip flexor rocks</h3><p>Use a split-kneeling position and gently shift forward without aggressively arching the lower back.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="pill orange">2 minutes</span><h3>Adductor rock-backs</h3><p>From hands and knees, extend one leg to the side and move the hips backward through a comfortable range.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="pill purple">2 minutes</span><h3>Supported deep squat shifts</h3><p>Hold a stable support, sit into a comfortable squat and shift gently from side to side.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="pill">2 minutes</span><h3>Hip airplanes with support</h3><p>Use a wall or chair and rotate the pelvis slowly over one leg to practice hip control.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="pill coral">1 minute</span><h3>Easy walking</h3><p>Finish by walking and noticing whether stride and hip motion feel different.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="movements">How to do the movements</h2>
  <h3>90/90 hip switches</h3>
  <p>Sit with both knees bent and feet on the floor. Let the knees rotate toward one side, then the other. Use your hands behind you for support if needed. The movement should feel smooth rather than forced.</p>

  <h3>Half-kneeling hip flexor rocks</h3>
  <p>Kneel on one knee with the opposite foot in front. Lightly tighten the glute on the kneeling side and shift the pelvis forward a small amount. Keep the ribs stacked rather than creating range by leaning backward.</p>

  <h3>Adductor rock-backs</h3>
  <p>Start on hands and knees, extend one leg to the side with the foot supported, then move the hips backward slowly. Stop before the position becomes sharp or unstable.</p>

  <h3>Supported squat shifts</h3>
  <p>Hold a sturdy counter, post or rack. Sit into the squat depth you can control, then gently move your weight toward one side and the other. Keep the feet planted and use the support to reduce effort.</p>

  <h3>Supported hip airplanes</h3>
  <p>Stand on one leg with a hand on a wall. Hinge slightly at the hip, then rotate the pelvis open and closed over the standing leg without rushing. This adds control to the range you have explored.</p>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Re-test after the routine.</strong> Walk, squat or perform the movement that felt restricted before. The goal is a useful change in comfort or control, not winning a flexibility contest.</div>

  <h2 id="before-after">Hip mobility before or after workouts?</h2>
  <p>Before training, choose dynamic movements that resemble the ranges you are about to use. If you are squatting, lunging or running, hip switches, rock-backs and supported squats can work as preparation.</p>

  <p>After training, lower-intensity mobility can be used as a cooldown if it feels good, but it is not mandatory. Walking, food, hydration and sleep often matter more for recovery than adding a long stretching session after every workout.</p>

  <h2 id="progress">How to progress hip mobility</h2>
  <p>Progress by improving control before chasing more range. You can gradually use less hand support, pause in positions, add light resistance or move more slowly through the same comfortable range.</p>

  <p>If a specific movement repeatedly causes pinching, numbness, radiating symptoms or pain that lingers, skip it and seek appropriate professional guidance rather than pushing deeper.</p>

  <h2>Pair mobility with strength</h2>
  <p>Hip mobility tends to be more useful when the ranges you gain are also used in strength training. Split squats, step-ups, squats, hinges and carries can help build control in positions that matter for daily life and exercise.</p>

  <p>For a broader recovery approach, see our guide to <a href="/recovery/mobility-recovery-after-workouts/">mobility and recovery after workouts</a>.</p>
</SEOArticle>
